        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          UV light can kill mold. The catch is in the word 'can'. Germicidal UV-C inactivates mold by damaging its DNA, and it does that reliably only when the light is strong, close and given time, often far more time than a spore spends near a lamp inside a purifier or a duct. Mold spores are some of the toughest things UV-C is asked to deal with, growth hidden in porous material never sees the light at all, and a dead spore is still an allergen. UV earns its place on an air conditioner coil, where it shines on the same surface all day. For the spores you breathe, filtration does the job and UV does not.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. 1. The Short Answer: Yes in a Lab, Rarely in Your Living Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ultraviolet light in the UV-C band, roughly 200 to 280 nanometres, is a proven disinfectant. It is used to treat drinking water, sterilise lab cabinets and keep commercial air handlers clean. Expose a mold spore to enough of it and the spore loses the ability to germinate. Nobody serious disputes that part. The dispute is over whether the UV device you are being sold delivers enough light, for long enough, to the mold that is actually causing your problem. In most homes the answer is no, and the reasons are physics rather than opinion.
          </p>

          <p>
            The EPA's position is short and blunt. It says UV germicidal irradiation cleaners, if properly designed, may destroy viruses, bacteria and some molds, but that typical UVGI cleaners used in homes have limited effectiveness against bacteria and molds, because effective destruction of most mold spores requires much higher UV exposure than a typical home unit provides. It adds a second point that matters even more for allergy sufferers: dead mold spores can still cause allergic reactions, so a device that kills spores without removing them may do little for symptoms.
          </p>

          <p>
            That leaves three different questions hiding inside 'does UV light kill mold'. Does a UV lamp in a portable purifier kill spores in the air passing through it? Mostly not, because the air is gone in a fraction of a second. Does a handheld UV wand or lamp kill mold on a wall? Only on the exposed surface, only at close range, and not in the hyphae growing into the material underneath. Does a UV lamp aimed at an air conditioner coil keep mold from growing there? Yes, and that is the use where the money is well spent.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">UV kills what it shines on, for as long as it shines on it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Dose is intensity multiplied by time. A lamp that bathes one surface around the clock builds an enormous dose. A lamp that a spore flies past in a tenth of a second builds almost none. Judge every UV product by that one question.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you came here because you are choosing a purifier and wondering whether to pay extra for a UV-C stage, the practical answer is that UV is a minor feature and true HEPA filtration is the part that matters. Our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best air purifiers for mold</a> rankings were built on that basis: we judged units on filtration, clean air delivery rate and carbon capacity first, and treated UV-C as a bonus that does no harm when the unit is certified ozone-safe. The rest of this guide explains why that ordering is the right one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-uv-c-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 2. How UV-C Kills Mold: Wavelength, DNA and Dose
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most germicidal lamps are low-pressure mercury tubes that emit almost all of their UV output at 253.7 nanometres. That wavelength sits close to the peak absorption of DNA and RNA, around 260 nanometres. When the light is absorbed, it causes adjacent bases in the genetic material, mainly thymines, to bond to each other, forming dimers that block the cell from copying its DNA. The organism is not burned, bleached or dissolved. It is 'inactivated', meaning it can no longer reproduce. For a mold spore, that means it cannot germinate and start a new colony.
          </p>

          <p>
            The amount of damage depends on dose, also called fluence, which is irradiance multiplied by exposure time. Irradiance is usually quoted in microwatts per square centimetre, and dose in millijoules per square centimetre. A strong lamp for a short time and a weak lamp for a long time can deliver the same dose. The figure that matters for any organism is the dose needed for a given kill, commonly expressed as 90 percent, one 'log', or 99 percent, two 'logs'. Each further log needs roughly the same increment again, so getting from 90 to 99.9 percent can take two or three times the dose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mold spores sit near the hard end of that scale. Published laboratory figures vary by species, strain and test method, but the commonly cited doses for 90 percent inactivation of Aspergillus species run from several tens to over 100 millijoules per square centimetre, with Aspergillus niger, a dark-spored mold, often listed above 100. Many common vegetative bacteria are knocked down by single-digit doses. Mold spores have thick walls and, in many indoor species such as Cladosporium, Alternaria and Aspergillus niger, dark melanin pigment that absorbs and screens UV before it reaches the DNA.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Target</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical UV-C dose for about 90% kill</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What that means in practice</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Many common vegetative bacteria</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low single digits, mJ/cm2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Easy target for a well-designed lamp</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bacterial spores</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher, often tens of mJ/cm2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Needs a deliberate, sustained dose</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mold spores (e.g. Aspergillus)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tens to over 100 mJ/cm2, depending on species</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Among the hardest airborne targets UV is asked to treat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mold growing inside drywall or wood</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not reachable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">UV-C does not penetrate the material at all</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            There is a further wrinkle. Many fungi carry repair enzymes, including photolyase, that can undo some UV damage when the organism is later exposed to visible light, a process called photoreactivation. Laboratory studies on fungal spores have documented it, which is one reason serious UV designers aim for a comfortable margin over the minimum dose rather than a bare 90 percent. A device that only just inactivates a spore in the test chamber may leave a fraction that recovers in a sunlit room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="contact-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. 3. The Contact Time Problem: Why Purifier and In-Duct UV Falls Short
          </h2>

          <p>
            Consider a UV lamp inside a portable air purifier. The fan moves several hundred cubic feet of air per minute through a chamber that is often only a few inches deep. A spore crossing that chamber spends well under a second in the beam, frequently a small fraction of a second. Even if the irradiance close to the tube is high, the dose a passing spore collects is tiny compared with the tens to hundreds of millijoules per square centimetre that mold spores need. The manufacturers of better units generally know this, which is why the honest ones describe UV-C as a supplement to filtration.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same arithmetic applies to a lamp mounted in a supply or return duct. Residential duct air commonly moves at several hundred feet per minute. If a lamp's useful beam covers about a foot of duct length, a spore at 600 feet per minute crosses it in a tenth of a second. Multiply an irradiance of, say, 1,000 microwatts per square centimetre by a tenth of a second and you get a dose of about 0.1 millijoules per square centimetre. That is hundreds of times short of what an Aspergillus spore needs. Real ducts vary, but not by the factor of several hundred needed to close that gap.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Arithmetic in One Line</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~0.1 s</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Time a spore spends passing an in-duct lamp</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~0.1 mJ</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rough dose collected, per cm2</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">100+ mJ</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Dose some mold spores need for 90% kill</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">8,000+ mJ</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Daily dose on a coil lit at 100 uW/cm2</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Then there is mixing. A purifier or HVAC system processes the room's air repeatedly, so in theory a spore gets many short exposures over an hour. In practice, each pass is so far below the lethal dose that repeated passes add up slowly, and a HEPA filter in the same machine removes the spore on its first pass anyway. That is the quiet irony of UV-C in a HEPA purifier: the filter catches the spore before the lamp can accumulate a meaningful dose on it, and any spore the lamp does inactivate is sitting in the filter either way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where UV-C is placed to shine on the filter surface rather than the moving air, it has a better case, because a trapped spore stays in the beam for days. That can stop growth on a filter that sits in damp air. But a properly maintained HEPA filter in a room at reasonable humidity is not a place mold thrives, and changing the filter on schedule achieves the same thing. If a purifier you like happens to include UV, such as the GermGuardian AC5900WCA in our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">mold purifier rankings</a>, there is no harm in it. Just buy it for the HEPA filter and the clean air delivery rate, which is what our <a href="/do-air-purifiers-help-with-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">guide to whether air purifiers help with mold</a> covers in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Products sold as UV 'air sanitizers' with no filter at all deserve the most scepticism. They depend entirely on the beam to do the work, and the contact-time problem applies in full. If a manufacturer claims a percentage kill of mold, ask what dose, what airflow, what species and what test chamber produced it. A figure measured in a sealed box over an hour does not describe a room where the same air passes the lamp once every few minutes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shadowing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 4. Shadows, Dust and Porous Surfaces: Spores vs Surface Growth
          </h2>

          <p>
            UV-C travels in straight lines and does not go through much. Ordinary window glass and most plastics block it, a layer of dust shields what is underneath, and paper, drywall, wood and fabric stop it within a fraction of a millimetre. Anything in a shadow, behind a fold of fabric, in a crevice or under another spore receives little or no dose. This is called shadowing, and it is why UV disinfection in hospitals is applied to cleaned, hard, exposed surfaces rather than to dirty or porous ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mold growth on a wall is not a coat of spores sitting on top. It is a colony of hyphae, thread-like filaments that grow into the material and feed on it. On drywall, the hyphae run through the paper facing; on wood, they follow the grain; in grout and caulk, they live in the pores. Shining UV on a moldy patch can inactivate some spores and surface hyphae that are directly lit, which may lighten the visible growth. The part of the colony inside the material is untouched, and as soon as there is moisture again it grows back.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why handheld UV wands and portable UV lamps are a poor mold tool. The beam weakens rapidly with distance, so a wand waved a few inches from a wall may deliver a small fraction of its rated surface dose, and only to what it is pointed at for those few seconds. The FDA has warned consumers about some UV wands that emit unsafe levels of radiation, and many have no independently verified output. Even a good one only addresses the exposed surface, which a damp cloth and detergent can clean faster and more completely.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SPORES VS COLONIES</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Airborne spores need filtration. Surface growth needs removal.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">UV is not the right tool for either. Spores in the air are best caught by a true HEPA filter. Growth on surfaces is best cleaned or cut out, and the moisture that fed it fixed, so it does not come back.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The EPA's long-standing guidance on cleanup is the better reference point. Growth covering roughly 10 square feet or less can usually be cleaned by a homeowner with gloves, eye protection and an N95 respirator, using detergent and water on hard surfaces and drying them fully. Porous materials that are badly colonised, such as ceiling tiles, carpet pad or drywall, generally need to be removed and replaced. Larger areas, or growth tied to sewage or contaminated water, are a job for a professional. If the problem is in a lower level, our guide on <a href="/how-to-get-rid-of-mold-in-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how to get rid of mold in a basement</a> walks through the full sequence.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dead mold is also still mold. Inactivating a spore does not remove the proteins and cell wall components that trigger allergic reactions, and it does not destroy mycotoxins that some species produce. A wall 'sterilised' with UV but left in place still sheds allergenic fragments into the room. That is the core reason UV, ozone and fogging treatments are not accepted substitutes for physical removal in professional remediation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coil-lamps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. 5. Where UV Genuinely Helps: HVAC Coil and Drain Pan Lamps
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is one place in a house where UV-C has the advantage instead of the handicap, and it is the cooling coil of an air conditioner or heat pump. During the cooling season the coil is cold and wet, the drain pan below it holds standing water, and dust lands on both. That combination grows biofilm, a slimy mix of bacteria and fungi that clogs the fins, narrows the airflow, reduces heat transfer and produces the musty 'dirty sock' smell some systems give off when they start up. A coil is a fixed target, and that changes everything.
          </p>

          <p>
            A lamp mounted to shine across the coil face irradiates the same surface for every hour it is on. ASHRAE's handbook guidance for coil irradiation calls for a minimum irradiance across the coil face in the region of 50 to 100 microwatts per square centimetre. That sounds small next to the figures in the previous section, but run the arithmetic: 100 microwatts per square centimetre for a full day is over 8,000 millijoules per square centimetre, tens of times more than even resistant mold spores need. Over days and weeks, nothing can establish itself on the lit surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the EPA's technical material on residential air cleaners notes that UV can destroy active growth on cooling coils while doing much less for spores in the moving airstream, and why the coil and drain pan are the preferred placement for residential UV lamps. Commercial buildings have used coil irradiation for years for the same reason. The benefit shows up as a coil that stays cleaner, maintains its airflow and capacity, and stops being a source of musty odor and of spores released into the ducts.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">UV placement</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Exposure time</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it can realistically do</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shining on the cooling coil and drain pan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Continuous, hours per day</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Prevent and clear biofilm and mold on the coil. The best use of UV in a home.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">In the duct, treating moving air</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A fraction of a second per pass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Little effect on mold spores. Some effect on easier targets.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Inside a portable purifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A fraction of a second per pass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A minor bonus. The HEPA filter does the real work.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Handheld wand or lamp on a wall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seconds, at variable distance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Surface only. Not a substitute for cleaning or removal.</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A coil lamp still has limits worth knowing before you pay for one. It needs to be installed where it lights the whole coil face, including the corners, and most need to run continuously rather than only when the blower runs. The lamps lose output over time even while they still glow, and manufacturers commonly rate them for around 9,000 hours, so plan on replacing the bulb about once a year. The tubes contain mercury and should be recycled. UV-C also degrades some plastics and wire insulation, so installers shield vulnerable components inside the air handler.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most of all, a coil lamp only treats the coil. It does not fix a duct system that is sweating in a humid attic, does not dry a damp basement, and does nothing about mold on walls or furniture. If your system smells musty and the coil is visibly fouled, have it cleaned first and then consider a lamp to keep it clean. If your house has a humidity problem, a dehumidifier will do more. Our comparison of <a href="/air-purifier-vs-dehumidifier/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">air purifiers and dehumidifiers</a> explains which one treats the cause.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ozone-bulbs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. 6. Ozone-Producing UV Bulbs and Other UV Hazards
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every UV lamp is the same. Low-pressure mercury lamps emit a second, shorter line at about 185 nanometres. Light at that wavelength splits oxygen molecules in the air, and the free oxygen atoms combine with oxygen to form ozone. Lamps sold as 'ozone-free' use a doped quartz envelope that absorbs the 185 nanometre line and passes only the 254 nanometre germicidal light. Lamps sold as 'ozone-producing', sometimes marketed as delivering extra odor or mold-killing power, deliberately let it through.
          </p>

          <p>
            For an occupied home, you want the ozone-free kind, without exception. Ozone is a lung irritant that causes coughing, chest tightness and throat irritation and worsens asthma. At the low concentrations that are considered safe to breathe, the EPA says it has little effect on mold or most indoor pollutants, and it reacts with chemicals from cleaning products, air fresheners and furnishings to form aldehydes and fine particles. Our companion piece on <a href="/do-ozone-generators-kill-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">whether ozone generators kill mold</a> covers the concentrations involved and why the answer for occupied spaces is no.
          </p>

          <p>
            California regulates this directly. Under the California Air Resources Board's air cleaner regulation, portable indoor air cleaning devices sold in the state since October 2010 must be certified to emit ozone at a concentration under 0.050 parts per million, and later amendments extended certification to electronic in-duct air cleaners. UV devices fall under the rule. Checking a UV purifier or in-duct lamp against CARB's certified list is a sensible step wherever you live, because a certified unit has at least been tested for ozone.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">CHECK BEFORE YOU BUY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Ozone-free lamp, CARB certified, and enclosed.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A UV product for a home should use an ozone-free lamp, appear on CARB's certified air cleaner list if it is an air cleaning device, and keep the UV-C light fully enclosed so no one can look at it.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The light itself is also a hazard. UV-C on unprotected skin causes a burn similar to sunburn, and on the eyes it causes photokeratitis, a painful inflammation of the cornea sometimes described as feeling like sand in the eyes, which can appear hours after exposure. Properly designed purifiers and coil lamps keep the light inside a closed housing, and good coil kits include a door switch that cuts the lamp when the air handler panel is opened. Open UV lamps and wands used around people and pets are where injuries happen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. 7. What Actually Removes Airborne Spores: HEPA, Moisture and Cleanup
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your goal is fewer mold spores in the air you breathe, the tool that does that is a true HEPA filter with enough airflow for the room. A true HEPA filter must capture at least 99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns, the size that is hardest for a fibrous filter to catch, and common indoor mold spores are several microns across, so they are captured at least that efficiently. Unlike UV, the filter does not depend on dose or exposure time. A spore that reaches the filter is held there until the filter is changed.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the most useful version of the question many people are really asking is not whether UV light kills mold, but which HEPA air purifier for mold spores will actually keep up with the room. The answer comes down to clean air delivery rate, not features. Aim for about five air changes per hour in a mold-prone room, which works out to a smoke CADR of roughly two-thirds of the floor area in square feet with an 8 foot ceiling. Our guide on <a href="/what-size-air-purifier-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">what size air purifier you need</a> shows the arithmetic for other ceiling heights.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then rank the candidates on what decides results: true HEPA rather than 'HEPA-type', a published CADR that meets your target on a fan speed you will tolerate, and a substantial activated carbon bed if the room smells musty, because the musty odor is gas that HEPA cannot catch. UV-C is fine as a tie-breaker if the unit is CARB certified, and irrelevant otherwise. That is exactly how our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best air purifier for mold</a> picks are ordered, from heavy-carbon units like the Austin Air HealthMate Plus down to an affordable bedroom option.
          </p>

          <p>
            A purifier still only treats the air. Mold needs moisture, and the fix that stops new growth is bringing indoor relative humidity to about 50 percent or below and keeping it there, plus fixing leaks and condensation. In basements, that usually means a correctly sized dehumidifier running alongside the purifier; our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best dehumidifiers for basements</a> guide covers the machines. Visible growth should be cleaned or removed, and settled spores dealt with by damp-wiping and a vacuum with sealed HEPA filtration.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put together, the plan looks like this. Find and fix the water. Clean or remove what is growing. Run a properly sized HEPA purifier to catch what is airborne during and after the work. If your central air smells musty, have the coil cleaned and consider a coil UV lamp to keep it that way. Skip UV wands, open germicidal lamps and anything that makes ozone. UV light does kill mold, but in a home it is a maintenance tool for one component, not a mold treatment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying Extra for UV-C Instead of CADR</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A UV stage on a purifier adds cost but little mold control, because air passes the lamp in a fraction of a second. The same money spent on a higher clean air delivery rate or a heavier carbon filter does far more. Treat UV-C as a tie-breaker between otherwise equal HEPA units, never as the deciding feature.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a UV Wand on Moldy Drywall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                UV-C cannot penetrate drywall paper, wood or grout, so it never reaches the hyphae growing inside. A wand may lighten the surface, then the colony regrows as soon as moisture returns. Clean hard surfaces with detergent and water, and cut out and replace porous material that is badly colonised.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an Ozone-Producing UV Bulb</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lamps that pass the 185 nanometre line make ozone, which irritates lungs and does little to mold at breathable levels. For any occupied home, choose an ozone-free lamp and check air cleaning devices against CARB's certified list. 'Extra purifying power' on a UV bulb's packaging usually means ozone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Dead Spores Are Harmless</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                UV inactivation stops a spore from germinating but leaves its allergenic proteins and any mycotoxins intact. A wall or room treated with UV and left uncleaned can still trigger symptoms. Removal, through filtration for the air and cleaning for surfaces, is what actually lowers exposure for sensitive people.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Coil Lamp Past Its Rated Life</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Germicidal tubes lose output long before they stop glowing, and many are rated for around 9,000 hours. A lamp left in for three years may still look lit while delivering too little dose to keep the coil clean. Replace it on the maker's schedule, roughly yearly, and recycle the old mercury tube.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does UV light kill mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, UV-C light can inactivate mold spores and surface growth if the dose is high enough, which means strong light, close range and enough time. Mold spores need much higher doses than most bacteria. Purifier and in-duct lamps expose passing air for a fraction of a second, so the EPA says typical home UV cleaners have limited effectiveness against mold.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does UV light kill mold spores in the air?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not reliably in typical home devices. A spore crosses a purifier or duct lamp in well under a second, collecting a tiny fraction of the dose mold spores need. Repeated passes help only slowly. A true HEPA filter catches airborne spores on the first pass regardless of dose, which is why filtration is the better tool for the air.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do UV lights in HVAC systems work for mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Coil-mounted UV lamps work well for one job: keeping the cooling coil and drain pan free of mold and biofilm, because the light shines on the same surface continuously. They do much less for spores moving through the ducts and nothing for mold elsewhere in the house. Expect to replace the bulb about once a year.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can UV light kill black mold on walls?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only on the exposed surface it directly lights, and only at close range. Black mold, like other molds, grows hyphae into drywall paper, wood and grout, where UV-C cannot reach. The colony regrows once moisture returns. Clean small areas with detergent and water, remove badly colonised porous material, and fix the moisture source.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sunlight kill mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sunlight helps, but not as a germicidal UV-C lamp would, because the atmosphere absorbs UV-C before it reaches the ground. Sunlight on a rug or cushion mostly works by drying the item, plus some UV-A and UV-B effect on exposed spores. It is useful after cleaning, but it will not kill mold growing deep in a material.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a UV-C air purifier better than HEPA for mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. HEPA physically removes spores from the air, while UV-C in a purifier has too little contact time to reliably inactivate them, and dead spores remain allergenic. A HEPA air purifier for mold spores, sized for about five air changes per hour, is the better buy. UV-C is acceptable as an extra if the unit is CARB certified.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are UV lights for mold safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Enclosed, ozone-free UV-C lamps in certified purifiers and properly installed coil kits are safe in normal use. Exposed UV-C light can burn skin and cause painful eye inflammation, and ozone-producing lamps irritate lungs. Avoid open germicidal lamps and wands around people or pets, and choose devices on CARB's certified air cleaner list.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

