        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            UV light does kill mold, but only with a dose that most home devices cannot deliver. A spore passing a purifier or duct lamp gets a fraction of a second of light, hidden growth gets none, and dead spores still cause allergic reactions. The one home application where UV clearly earns its cost is a coil lamp that keeps the air conditioner's cooling coil and drain pan clean.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For the spores you breathe, buy filtration, not light: a true HEPA purifier sized for about five air changes an hour, with carbon if the room smells musty, running alongside a fix for the moisture. Start with our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best air purifiers for mold</a>.
          </p>
